# How LLM Code Assistants Generate and Apply Diffs in the IDE

## Diff Generation from LLM Suggestions  
AI coding assistants like **Cursor**, **Sourcegraph Cody**, and **Continue.dev** all take an original code snippet and an AI-proposed update, then compute a *diff* to show what changed. Under the hood, they use standard text diff techniques (line-by-line comparisons, typically a Myers or similar algorithm) to identify additions and deletions between the old and new code. For example, Cursor’s “Inline editing” feature lets you select code and ask for a change; behind the scenes it runs a foundation model (GPT or Claude) to produce the modified code and then presents a *“simple diff view”* of the selected code vs. the AI-edited code ([How I write code using Cursor: A review](https://www.arguingwithalgorithms.com/posts/cursor-review.html#:~:text=provide%20code%20completion%20in%20the,to%20free%20and%20paid%20users)). The assistants may either instruct the LLM to output a unified diff or (more often) have the extension compute the diff itself by comparing the original and new text. 

In **Continue.dev**, the VS Code extension streams the AI’s answer and updates a side-by-side diff in real-time. As the LLM generates new code, Continue continuously compares it with the original and *displays a live diff* of the changes ([Show HN: Continue – Open-source coding autopilot | Hacker News](https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=36882146#:~:text=One%20example%20is%20displaying%20a,as%20we%20stream%20an%20edit)). (This is done line-by-line; one comment from the Continue team notes that *“displaying a side-by-side diff as we stream an edit”* is a key feature of their approach ([Show HN: Continue – Open-source coding autopilot | Hacker News](https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=36882146#:~:text=One%20example%20is%20displaying%20a,as%20we%20stream%20an%20edit)).) In practice, early versions of Continue took a simple approach: if you highlighted code and asked for an edit, the AI’s suggested replacement would often be shown as the entire block removed and replaced, rather than isolating only the small differences. In other words, the *“generated diff tends to be 100% change of the selected code”* instead of a minimal diff ([Continue.dev: The Swiss Army Knife That Sometimes Fails to Cut - DEV Community](https://dev.to/maximsaplin/continuedev-the-swiss-army-knife-that-sometimes-fails-to-cut-4gg3#:~:text=,changes%20do%20not%20get%20isolated)). This happens when the model rewrites the whole snippet. The Continue developers have been improving this by exploring unified diff outputs – there’s even a feature request to have the LLM produce a patch that the tool can apply directly (to avoid regenerating large sections of code) ([Create a new "Patch file" Tool · Issue #3554 · continuedev/continue · GitHub](https://github.com/continuedev/continue/issues/3554#:~:text=Currently%20the%20only%20way%20to,tokens%20and%20is%20rather%20expensive)). 

Cody’s approach is to generate diffs that are as precise as possible. When you use Cody’s **Inline Edit** (for example, highlight code and press the “Edit” hotkey), *“Cody will generate a diff for the change in seconds.”* ([Cody Quickstart - Sourcegraph docs](https://docs.sourcegraph.com/cody/quickstart#:~:text=You%20can%20refactor%20your%20code,for%20the%20change%20in%20seconds)) ([Cody Quickstart - Sourcegraph docs](https://docs.sourcegraph.com/cody/quickstart#:~:text=Type%20your%20refactoring%20suggestion%20in,are%20unsatisfied%20with%20the%20result)) Internally, Cody often just requests the new code from the model (not necessarily a pre-formatted diff) and then computes the diff between the old and new snippet. In some cases – especially when using the chat interface – Cody may ask the LLM to produce a unified diff. However, relying on the raw LLM diff can be tricky: if the model includes incorrect line numbers or context, the patch might not apply cleanly. In fact, users reported that *“Cody creates diff files that sometimes aren’t even correct… with all these `+`s and `-`s… and `@@` headers”*, especially for large files ([Apply button isn't useful · Issue #6584 · sourcegraph/cody · GitHub](https://github.com/sourcegraph/cody/issues/6584#:~:text=Cody%20create%20diff%20files%20that,It%20didn%27t)). This indicates the model was returning a unified diff (with Git-style hunks) that didn’t match perfectly. To handle this, Cody’s extension will parse the diff output and attempt to apply it, or fall back to showing it to the user for confirmation. Recent versions of Cody actually avoid the raw diff approach for big changes – instead, they fetch the file content, let the model propose edits, then compute the diff in the client for display. In summary, all these tools use classic diff generation (textual comparison of old vs new code). They may use libraries or built-in functions equivalent to Unix `diff`/Git diff. (Cursor, being a VS Code fork, likely leverages VS Code’s internal diff engine for this.)

## Patch Application in the IDE  
Once a diff is generated and shown to the user, the next step is applying those changes to the actual codebase when the user accepts them. These assistants integrate with IDE APIs to programmatically apply edits. In VS Code, this means using the TextEditor or WorkspaceEdit APIs to replace text in the open file. For example, Sourcegraph **Cody**’s “Smart Apply” feature lets you take a code suggestion from the chat and insert it into your file with one click. When you hit **Apply**, *“Cody will analyze your open code file, find where that relevant code should live, and add a diff.”* ([Chat - Sourcegraph docs](https://sourcegraph.com/docs/cody/capabilities/chat#:~:text=Cody%20lets%20you%20dynamically%20insert,chat%20suggestions%20to%20written%20code)) In practical terms, Cody locates the correct position (using context from the suggestion or AST hints) and then creates a patch. Initially, Cody showed the patch as ghost text (a preview) and only applied it upon confirmation. In versions around mid-2024, clicking “Open Diff” in Cody would actually commit the changes into a temporary diff view. This led to confusion where the diff view **automatically accepted** the changes (the ghost text became real text) ([VS Code: Clicking "Open Diff" will accept the changes automatically and ghost text disappear · Issue #4879 · sourcegraph/cody · GitHub](https://github.com/sourcegraph/cody/issues/4879#:~:text=5,check%20the%20original%20sourcecode%20file)) ([VS Code: Clicking "Open Diff" will accept the changes automatically and ghost text disappear · Issue #4879 · sourcegraph/cody · GitHub](https://github.com/sourcegraph/cody/issues/4879#:~:text=Clicking%20on%20,automatically%20accept%20the%20changes)). The Cody team acknowledged this was by design but not ideal, and they later refined the workflow to require an explicit accept action ([VS Code: Clicking "Open Diff" will accept the changes automatically and ghost text disappear · Issue #4879 · sourcegraph/cody · GitHub](https://github.com/sourcegraph/cody/issues/4879#:~:text=justinmilner1%20%20%20commented%20,70)). Now, Cody’s inline diff (described below) allows the user to accept or reject the edit, and only on accept does it finalize the modification in the file.

**Continue.dev** and **Cursor** follow a similar pattern. In Continue, after you review the “Continue Diff” side-by-side, you can press an **✔ Accept** button (or a command) to apply the change. Under the hood, the extension then performs the edits on the actual file buffer. The diff view in Continue is essentially read-only preview – the real file isn’t modified until you accept. (If you reject, no changes are made.) Continue implements this by constructing an edit script from the diff (essentially, remove these lines, add those lines) and using VS Code’s workspace edits to apply it. Because Continue is open source, we know it opens the diff using a custom scheme/document; once accepted, it writes the new content back to the original file. Cursor, being a full IDE (forked from VS Code), can manage this more seamlessly. When you accept a suggested edit in Cursor’s diff view, it directly replaces the old code with the AI’s new code in your editor. Cursor’s architecture even uses a *“shadow workspace”* for agent-driven iterative changes – effectively a background copy of your project where it can apply and test AI changes safely ([Iterating with Shadow Workspaces | Cursor - The AI Code Editor](https://www.cursor.com/blog/shadow-workspace#:~:text=But%20naively%20letting%20AIs%20run,without%20affecting%20your%20coding%20experience)) ([Iterating with Shadow Workspaces | Cursor - The AI Code Editor](https://www.cursor.com/blog/shadow-workspace#:~:text=What%20we%20really%20want%20is,level%20independence)). Once the AI’s diff is approved by the user, those changes can be merged into the real workspace. This design ensures that your actual code is only changed when you say so, preventing the AI from corrupting your files without review.

All these tools make use of the editor’s capabilities to apply patches. In VS Code, that means the extension might call `vscode.workspace.applyEdit(...)` with a set of text edits, or open an editor with the new code and let you save it. In JetBrains IDEs (which Cody and Continue support as well), the plugins use a similar approach via the JetBrains OpenAPI: they can create a diff window and, on accept, call the IDE’s document update methods to apply the patch. In short, **the diff you see is a preview**, and behind it the assistant holds the new code ready. Approving merges it into the file system (often by writing to the buffer, which you then save to disk), whereas canceling simply drops the suggestion.

## Diff Display in the Editor UI  
A crucial part of the workflow is **how the diff is shown to the user**. All three assistants leverage familiar diff UIs to make the changes clear:

- **Side-by-side or Inline Diff Views:** Continue and early versions of Cody use the IDE’s native diff viewer, which typically shows two panels (original on the left, new on the right) with colored highlights. In VS Code, the extension can invoke the built-in diff editor by opening two virtual documents – one with the old code, one with the new – and calling the `vscode.diff` command. This is exactly what Continue does: after you prompt for an edit, *“Wait for the Continue Diff to open”* ([Support: Continue Diff causes Rust Anlayzer to crash · Issue #1409 · continuedev/continue · GitHub](https://github.com/continuedev/continue/issues/1409#:~:text=When%20using%20the%20Continue%20extension,when%20performing%20the%20following%20steps)) and you’ll see a diff view appear. This is the same interface VS Code uses for Git diffs, so it supports inline or side-by-side layouts, syntax coloring, and navigation between changes. Continue even streams into this view, so you can watch the diff materialize as the AI writes code. In a Hacker News discussion, the Continue author describes that the extension is *displaying the diff as we stream an edit* in real-time ([Show HN: Continue – Open-source coding autopilot | Hacker News](https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=36882146#:~:text=One%20example%20is%20displaying%20a,as%20we%20stream%20an%20edit)). Users have noted that this experience is *“pretty well done”*, as you can literally see the code being edited step by step ([Show HN: Continue – Open-source coding autopilot | Hacker News](https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=36882146#:~:text=match%20at%20L468%20diff%20with,I%27m%20lazy)). Once generation is done, you typically have controls to accept or reject. In Continue’s case, small **Accept/Reject** buttons are shown (the dev community feedback noted these were somewhat hard to see in early versions ([Continue.dev: The Swiss Army Knife That Sometimes Fails to Cut - DEV Community](https://dev.to/maximsaplin/continuedev-the-swiss-army-knife-that-sometimes-fails-to-cut-4gg3#:~:text=Image%3A%20Image%20description))). Cursor’s Composer (for multi-file changes) similarly presents a sequence of diff views for each file, rather than applying everything blindly. You can review each file’s changes one by one in Cursor’s custom UI, which is modeled after VS Code’s diff view (with clear visual diffs and the ability to approve changes) ([How I write code using Cursor: A review](https://www.arguingwithalgorithms.com/posts/cursor-review.html#:~:text=Claude,to%20free%20and%20paid%20users)).

- **Inline Diffs and Ghost Text:** Sourcegraph Cody has evolved from using a separate diff panel to an inline diff approach. Initially, Cody would insert **ghost text** (grayed-out suggestion text) in your editor for small edits and provide a “Show Diff” link for larger changes. The ghost text was essentially an inline preview of the insertion. With Cody v1.24 (June 2024), the team introduced *“inline diffs directly in your file”* with a smooth animation ([Cody for VS Code v1.24: Inline diffs for code edits, context chips, and Claude 3.5 Sonnet | Sourcegraph Blog](https://sourcegraph.com/blog/cody-vscode-1-24-0-release#:~:text=Inline%20diff%20view%20and%20animation,for%20Cody%E2%80%99s%20inline%20edits)). Now, when Cody finishes an edit, your editor highlights the modified lines in green or red **in situ**. For example, if you refactor a function with Cody, as soon as the edit is done you’ll see the new code inserted and the removed code struck out or highlighted, all in the actual file buffer (not a secondary panel). This inline diff is accompanied by UI controls (like an ❌ to revert or a ✅ to accept) – so you can either keep the changes or undo them. The inline approach makes it very clear *“exactly what happened”* and lets you continue coding without switching context ([Cody for VS Code v1.24: Inline diffs for code edits, context chips, and Claude 3.5 Sonnet | Sourcegraph Blog](https://sourcegraph.com/blog/cody-vscode-1-24-0-release#:~:text=When%20you%20run%20a%20command,accept%20or%20decline%20the%20edit)). It’s similar to how an IDE might show live diffs for applied refactoring, except here it’s driven by the AI. Under the hood, Cody likely uses VS Code *decorations* or the new inline progress API to render these changes. The mention of *“progressive animation as Cody makes edits”* ([Cody for VS Code v1.24: Inline diffs for code edits, context chips, and Claude 3.5 Sonnet | Sourcegraph Blog](https://sourcegraph.com/blog/cody-vscode-1-24-0-release#:~:text=Inline%20diff%20view%20and%20animation,for%20Cody%E2%80%99s%20inline%20edits)) suggests that you might see text being inserted one chunk at a time (mimicking how the AI wrote it) and then the final highlighted diff. This is a purely client-side presentation; by the time it’s shown, the new code is already in an unsaved state in your editor (waiting for you to accept or revert).

- **Native Diff Tools Integration:** Since Cursor is a fork of VS Code, it doesn’t need to reinvent the diff UI – it reuses it but tightly integrates the workflow. When you use Cursor’s inline edit chat (triggered via a keyboard shortcut or command), the editor swaps to a diff mode for the selected range. You see original and modified code with plus/minus highlights just as you would in a Git diff, but scoped to that function or block you edited. Users have praised Cursor’s diff UX; one review noted that Cursor provides a clear diff before applying changes, whereas some other tools (like the early version of Bolt) did not, *“reducing control over code modifications”* ([Cursor AI, v0, and Bolt.new: An Honest Comparison of Today's AI ...](https://carlrannaberg.medium.com/cursor-ai-v0-and-bolt-new-an-honest-comparison-of-todays-ai-coding-tools-b4277e1eb1f9#:~:text=Cursor%20AI%2C%20v0%2C%20and%20Bolt,While)). This emphasis on showing diffs is becoming standard – it gives developers confidence by letting them inspect AI changes. Even JetBrains-based assistants (like Cody for JetBrains or Continue’s IntelliJ plugin) hook into the IDE’s diff viewer. JetBrains IDEs have a built-in diff/merge tool for version control, and the plugins invoke that to show AI edits. In fact, the Continue JetBrains plugin description explicitly says: *“highlight a block of code, describe your changes, and a diff will be streamed inline”* ([Continue - IntelliJ IDEs Plugin - JetBrains Marketplace](https://plugins.jetbrains.com/plugin/22707-continue#:~:text=Marketplace%20plugins,will%20be%20streamed%20inline)), which parallels the VS Code behavior.

In all cases, the diff views leverage syntax highlighting and context lines to make the AI’s changes easy to review. These aren’t just plain text dumps – they use the same rendering as manual diffs. For instance, if the AI only changed one line inside a 20-line function, the diff will show a few context lines around it (not the entire file), with that one line marked in red/green. This is either computed by the assistant or (if the LLM provided a unified diff format) taken from the model’s output. When models output diffs, they use the unified diff format with `@@ ... @@` headers and `+/-` lines, which the extensions can convert into this view. However, as noted, some assistants found it more reliable to compute the diff themselves to avoid mistakes in those headers ([Apply button isn't useful · Issue #6584 · sourcegraph/cody · GitHub](https://github.com/sourcegraph/cody/issues/6584#:~:text=Cody%20create%20diff%20files%20that,It%20didn%27t)).

## Notable Open-Source Implementations and Plugins  
Both **Continue.dev** and **Sourcegraph Cody** are open-source, so their code provides insight into these mechanisms. The Continue VS Code extension code shows commands for opening diff editors and applying edits, and discussions on its issue tracker detail plans for using patch-based workflows ([Create a new "Patch file" Tool · Issue #3554 · continuedev/continue · GitHub](https://github.com/continuedev/continue/issues/3554#:~:text=Currently%20the%20only%20way%20to,tokens%20and%20is%20rather%20expensive)). Sourcegraph’s Cody repo similarly has logic for diff generation and insertion. (Cody even uses a parsing library called Tree-sitter in some cases to better understand the code’s structure when applying changes ([How Cody understands your codebase | Sourcegraph Blog](https://sourcegraph.com/blog/how-cody-understands-your-codebase#:~:text=How%20Cody%20understands%20your%20codebase,into%20the%20active%20workflow%2C)), though actual diffing still relies on textual comparison.)

Outside of IDE plugins, the *“unified diff”* approach is also used by CLI-based AI code tools. For example, **Aider** (an AI pair-programming CLI) explicitly prompts GPT-4 to return changes as unified diffs, and then it applies those patches with `git apply` or Python’s difflib. This method was found to significantly improve the quality of GPT-4’s edits. In the Aider project’s own words: *“Aider now asks GPT-4 Turbo to use unified diffs to edit your code.”* ([Unified diffs make GPT-4 Turbo 3X less lazy | aider](https://aider.chat/docs/unified-diffs.html#:~:text=Aider%20now%20asks%20GPT,comments%20like%20%E2%80%9C%E2%80%A6add%20logic%20here%E2%80%A6%E2%80%9D)) The diff acts as an intermediate language that the tool can reliably interpret – only the exact intended changes are made, nothing more. This is similar to what was proposed for Continue (as a “Patch file” tool) and is generally a best practice for safe AI-driven refactors. Another open plugin, **GitHub Copilot Labs**, offered an experimental *“Fix/Refactor”* feature where the AI suggestion was shown in a diff-like panel before application. While not as fully developed, it underscores the trend: *AI assistants present changes as diffs so developers remain in control*. 

In conclusion, AI coding assistants convert raw LLM suggestions into familiar diff views using the standard toolkit of diff algorithms and IDE APIs. They generate a textual delta (via algorithms like Myers or by leveraging the LLM to format a patch) between the original and new code, then use the IDE’s diff viewer (or inline decoration capabilities) to display it. Finally, with the user’s approval, they apply those changes through the editor’s normal edit/undo stack – just as if a developer accepted a patch in a code review tool. This end-to-end process — **diff generation**, **diff preview, and **patch application** — is what enables tools like Cursor, Cody, and Continue to safely integrate AI-proposed code updates into your day-to-day development workflow ([How I write code using Cursor: A review](https://www.arguingwithalgorithms.com/posts/cursor-review.html#:~:text=provide%20code%20completion%20in%20the,to%20free%20and%20paid%20users)) ([Cody for VS Code v1.24: Inline diffs for code edits, context chips, and Claude 3.5 Sonnet | Sourcegraph Blog](https://sourcegraph.com/blog/cody-vscode-1-24-0-release#:~:text=Inline%20diff%20view%20and%20animation,for%20Cody%E2%80%99s%20inline%20edits)).

